1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,386
The most famous name in electric cars belongs

2
00:00:03,386 --> 00:00:05,472
to a man who died broke.

3
00:00:05,722 --> 00:00:08,156
January 7th, 1943.

4
00:00:08,156 --> 00:00:09,262
New York City.

5
00:00:09,262 --> 00:00:12,402
In room 3327 of the Hotel New Yorker, an

6
00:00:12,402 --> 00:00:16,612
86-year-old inventor dies alone, deep in debt.

7
00:00:16,862 --> 00:00:19,445
Sixty years later, a startup in California

8
00:00:19,445 --> 00:00:21,351
puts his name on a car company.

9
00:00:21,351 --> 00:00:23,862
Today, it's one of the most valuable car

10
00:00:23,862 --> 00:00:25,655
companies on Earth.

11
00:00:25,905 --> 00:00:27,308
And the strangest part?

12
00:00:27,308 --> 00:00:29,691
For years, the company named after him didn't

13
00:00:29,691 --> 00:00:31,226
even own its own name online.

14
00:00:31,226 --> 00:00:32,987
We'll get to that.

15
00:00:33,237 --> 00:00:35,259
This is the story of Nikola Tesla.

16
00:00:35,259 --> 00:00:37,881
The man, the myths, and the brand.

17
00:00:42,431 --> 00:00:45,233
Nikola Tesla was born in 1856, in a small

18
00:00:45,233 --> 00:00:48,241
village in what is now Croatia, to a Serbian

19
00:00:48,241 --> 00:00:48,720
family.

20
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:50,375
His father was a priest.

21
00:00:50,375 --> 00:00:53,173
His mother, he said, invented gadgets for the

22
00:00:53,173 --> 00:00:54,395
household.

23
00:00:54,645 --> 00:00:57,249
He studied engineering, worked for telephone

24
00:00:57,249 --> 00:00:59,972
and electric companies in Europe, and in 1884,

25
00:00:59,972 --> 00:01:02,635
he sailed to New York, with almost nothing in

26
00:01:02,635 --> 00:01:03,945
his pockets.

27
00:01:04,195 --> 00:01:05,771
He had a remarkable memory.

28
00:01:05,771 --> 00:01:08,141
He claimed he could picture entire machines in

29
00:01:08,141 --> 00:01:10,511
his head, and test them there, before building

30
00:01:10,511 --> 00:01:11,574
anything.

31
00:01:11,824 --> 00:01:15,139
He landed a job working for Thomas Edison.

32
00:01:15,389 --> 00:01:18,154
And this is where the first famous story comes

33
00:01:18,154 --> 00:01:18,335
in.

34
00:01:18,335 --> 00:01:21,005
According to Tesla, Edison promised him

35
00:01:21,005 --> 00:01:23,333
$50,000 to improve his generators.

36
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:25,555
When Tesla delivered, Edison supposedly

37
00:01:25,555 --> 00:01:27,949
laughed, and told him he didn't understand

38
00:01:27,949 --> 00:01:29,404
American humor.

39
00:01:29,654 --> 00:01:32,219
That story comes from Tesla himself, and some

40
00:01:32,219 --> 00:01:33,873
historians doubt the details.

41
00:01:33,873 --> 00:01:36,868
But either way, he quit within the year.

42
00:01:37,118 --> 00:01:39,574
His first company of his own pushed him out.

43
00:01:39,574 --> 00:01:41,713
By his own account, he ended up digging

44
00:01:41,713 --> 00:01:43,465
ditches for a living.

45
00:01:45,615 --> 00:01:48,254
But Tesla had an idea he'd carried since his

46
00:01:48,254 --> 00:01:50,954
student days: a motor that ran on alternating

47
00:01:50,954 --> 00:01:53,353
current, with no brushes and no sparking

48
00:01:53,353 --> 00:01:56,173
contacts, almost nothing to wear out.

49
00:01:56,423 --> 00:01:58,866
The trick was something called a rotating

50
00:01:58,866 --> 00:01:59,760
magnetic field.

51
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:02,297
Feed alternating currents into coils around a

52
00:02:02,297 --> 00:02:04,721
ring, slightly out of step with each other,

53
00:02:04,721 --> 00:02:06,751
and the magnetic field itself spins.

54
00:02:06,751 --> 00:02:09,462
Put a metal rotor in the middle, and the field

55
00:02:09,462 --> 00:02:11,005
drags it around.

56
00:02:11,255 --> 00:02:14,195
In 1888, he patented his AC induction motor,

57
00:02:14,195 --> 00:02:16,666
and presented it to the country's top

58
00:02:16,666 --> 00:02:18,669
electrical engineers.

59
00:02:18,919 --> 00:02:21,160
One man was paying very close attention:

60
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:22,280
George Westinghouse.

61
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:24,739
He licensed Tesla's patents, and hired him.

62
00:02:24,739 --> 00:02:26,043
Remember this motor.

63
00:02:26,043 --> 00:02:29,152
It's coming back, more than a century later.

64
00:02:31,502 --> 00:02:33,113
There was just one problem.

65
00:02:33,113 --> 00:02:35,441
Edison had bet everything on direct current,

66
00:02:35,441 --> 00:02:38,157
and he wasn't giving up without a fight.

67
00:02:38,407 --> 00:02:41,156
It became known as the War of the Currents.

68
00:02:41,156 --> 00:02:43,869
Edison's camp warned that alternating current

69
00:02:43,869 --> 00:02:46,342
was deadly, and pointed to its use in the

70
00:02:46,342 --> 00:02:48,208
first electric chair.

71
00:02:48,458 --> 00:02:51,284
An engineer allied with Edison's side even

72
00:02:51,284 --> 00:02:54,379
electrocuted animals in public demonstrations,

73
00:02:54,379 --> 00:02:57,267
to show how dangerous AC could be.

74
00:02:57,517 --> 00:02:59,587
Tesla fought back with showmanship.

75
00:02:59,587 --> 00:03:02,091
In his lectures, he let high-frequency current

76
00:03:02,091 --> 00:03:04,485
pass through his own body, lighting up lamps

77
00:03:04,485 --> 00:03:05,793
in his hands.

78
00:03:06,043 --> 00:03:07,881
But AC had a huge advantage.

79
00:03:07,881 --> 00:03:10,506
It could be sent long distances efficiently.

80
00:03:10,506 --> 00:03:13,312
Direct current, back then, couldn't.

81
00:03:13,562 --> 00:03:16,742
In 1893, Westinghouse lit up the Chicago

82
00:03:16,742 --> 00:03:17,776
World's Fair.

83
00:03:17,776 --> 00:03:20,465
Millions of visitors saw a glowing white city

84
00:03:20,465 --> 00:03:22,918
running on alternating current.

85
00:03:23,168 --> 00:03:24,770
Then came Niagara Falls.

86
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:27,686
In 1896, power generated at the falls, using

87
00:03:27,686 --> 00:03:30,601
Tesla's system, reached the city of Buffalo,

88
00:03:30,601 --> 00:03:32,792
more than 20 miles away.

89
00:03:33,042 --> 00:03:34,496
Alternating current won.

90
00:03:34,496 --> 00:03:37,005
It's the kind of power coming out of your wall

91
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:38,151
right now.

92
00:03:38,401 --> 00:03:41,135
So Tesla should have been rich, right?

93
00:03:44,685 --> 00:03:46,359
Here's where it goes wrong.

94
00:03:46,359 --> 00:03:48,943
Westinghouse's company ran into serious money

95
00:03:48,943 --> 00:03:49,402
trouble.

96
00:03:49,402 --> 00:03:52,859
Tesla agreed to give up his royalties, and in

97
00:03:52,859 --> 00:03:56,392
1897, he took a lump sum of about $216,000 for

98
00:03:56,392 --> 00:03:57,914
his patents.

99
00:03:58,164 --> 00:04:00,920
That was a lot of money in 1897.

100
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:02,974
But it was a tiny slice of what those

101
00:04:02,974 --> 00:04:05,128
royalties could have become.

102
00:04:05,378 --> 00:04:08,287
And in 1895, a fire tore through his New York

103
00:04:08,287 --> 00:04:11,196
laboratory, destroying years of equipment and

104
00:04:11,196 --> 00:04:12,184
notes.

105
00:04:12,434 --> 00:04:14,985
Then Tesla went all in on his biggest dream:

106
00:04:14,985 --> 00:04:15,855
wireless power.

107
00:04:15,855 --> 00:04:19,053
On Long Island, he built a giant tower called

108
00:04:19,053 --> 00:04:22,108
Wardenclyffe, funded with $150,000 from the

109
00:04:22,108 --> 00:04:22,748
banker J.

110
00:04:22,748 --> 00:04:23,163
P.

111
00:04:23,163 --> 00:04:24,436
Morgan.

112
00:04:24,686 --> 00:04:27,224
The plan was to send messages, and eventually

113
00:04:27,224 --> 00:04:29,706
power, through the air and the earth itself.

114
00:04:29,706 --> 00:04:32,186
But the money ran out, and Morgan wouldn't

115
00:04:32,186 --> 00:04:33,495
invest more.

116
00:04:33,745 --> 00:04:35,812
The tower never did what he hoped.

117
00:04:35,812 --> 00:04:38,685
Tesla lost the property to his creditors, and

118
00:04:38,685 --> 00:04:42,095
in 1917, the tower was demolished for scrap.

119
00:04:42,345 --> 00:04:44,839
For the rest of his life, Tesla moved from

120
00:04:44,839 --> 00:04:47,334
hotel to hotel, often behind on the bills,

121
00:04:47,334 --> 00:04:49,828
feeding pigeons, and announcing inventions

122
00:04:49,828 --> 00:04:51,616
that never appeared.

123
00:04:51,866 --> 00:04:54,543
He even told his biographer that he loved one

124
00:04:54,543 --> 00:04:57,642
white pigeon, the way a man loves a woman.

125
00:04:59,992 --> 00:05:02,133
And that's where the myths start piling up.

126
00:05:02,133 --> 00:05:03,913
Let's sort out a few.

127
00:05:04,163 --> 00:05:06,558
Myth number one: Tesla tore up a contract

128
00:05:06,558 --> 00:05:08,544
worth billions, to save his friend

129
00:05:08,544 --> 00:05:09,303
Westinghouse.

130
00:05:09,303 --> 00:05:12,024
What we can document is the buyout: a lump sum

131
00:05:12,024 --> 00:05:13,265
instead of royalties.

132
00:05:13,265 --> 00:05:16,006
The dramatic tearing moment is much harder to

133
00:05:16,006 --> 00:05:16,971
prove.

134
00:05:17,221 --> 00:05:19,497
Myth number two: he built a death ray.

135
00:05:19,497 --> 00:05:22,322
In the 1930s, he told reporters he'd designed

136
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:25,084
a beam that could stop armies from far away.

137
00:05:25,084 --> 00:05:27,960
There's no evidence he ever built one.

138
00:05:28,210 --> 00:05:30,968
Myth number three: the Supreme Court declared

139
00:05:30,968 --> 00:05:32,684
Tesla the inventor of radio.

140
00:05:32,684 --> 00:05:35,760
In 1943, the court did knock out key parts of

141
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:38,904
Marconi's radio patent, citing earlier work by

142
00:05:38,904 --> 00:05:40,066
Tesla and others.

143
00:05:40,066 --> 00:05:42,643
But it never named Tesla the inventor of

144
00:05:42,643 --> 00:05:43,630
radio.

145
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:46,343
And the big one: Tesla invented everything.

146
00:05:46,343 --> 00:05:47,147
He didn't.

147
00:05:47,147 --> 00:05:49,604
But he did help build the electric world we

148
00:05:49,604 --> 00:05:50,061
live in.

149
00:05:50,061 --> 00:05:52,475
There's even a scientific unit of magnetism

150
00:05:52,475 --> 00:05:54,029
called the tesla.

151
00:05:54,279 --> 00:05:57,372
He died in 1943, in that hotel room.

152
00:05:57,372 --> 00:06:00,072
Afterward, the government took his belongings

153
00:06:00,072 --> 00:06:01,332
and papers.

154
00:06:03,882 --> 00:06:05,832
Now fast forward sixty years.

155
00:06:05,832 --> 00:06:07,893
July 1st, 2003.

156
00:06:07,893 --> 00:06:10,552
Two engineers in California, Martin Eberhard

157
00:06:10,552 --> 00:06:13,091
and Marc Tarpenning, start an electric car

158
00:06:13,091 --> 00:06:14,175
company.

159
00:06:14,425 --> 00:06:16,956
Eberhard is credited with choosing the name:

160
00:06:16,956 --> 00:06:17,704
Tesla Motors.

161
00:06:17,704 --> 00:06:19,344
A tribute to the inventor.

162
00:06:19,344 --> 00:06:21,754
But also a perfect fit, because their first

163
00:06:21,754 --> 00:06:23,828
car, the Roadster, would run on an AC

164
00:06:23,828 --> 00:06:24,725
induction motor.

165
00:06:24,725 --> 00:06:28,861
The same kind of motor Tesla patented in 1888.

166
00:06:29,111 --> 00:06:31,571
Elon Musk joined in 2004.

167
00:06:31,571 --> 00:06:34,778
He led the first big funding round, with $6.5

168
00:06:34,778 --> 00:06:38,056
million of his own money, and became chairman.

169
00:06:38,056 --> 00:06:40,389
Later, he became the CEO.

170
00:06:40,639 --> 00:06:43,807
The Roadster finally hit the road in 2008.

171
00:06:43,807 --> 00:06:47,115
Only about 2,500 were ever built.

172
00:06:47,365 --> 00:06:50,014
It was built on a modified Lotus sports car

173
00:06:50,014 --> 00:06:52,478
chassis, packed with thousands of small,

174
00:06:52,478 --> 00:06:54,741
laptop-style battery cells.

175
00:06:54,991 --> 00:06:56,436
And then it got messy.

176
00:06:56,436 --> 00:06:57,468
Eberhard sued.

177
00:06:57,468 --> 00:07:00,243
The settlement, in 2009, allowed five people

178
00:07:00,243 --> 00:07:03,745
to call themselves co-founders, Musk included.

179
00:07:03,995 --> 00:07:06,173
Here's the part that sounds made up.

180
00:07:06,173 --> 00:07:08,595
For years, the company didn't even own

181
00:07:08,595 --> 00:07:09,233
tesla.com.

182
00:07:09,233 --> 00:07:12,300
That address had belonged to a man named Stu

183
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:13,693
Grossman since 1992.

184
00:07:13,693 --> 00:07:16,338
Tesla finally got it in 2016.

185
00:07:16,338 --> 00:07:19,329
Musk later said it cost $11 million, and took

186
00:07:19,329 --> 00:07:21,191
more than a decade.

187
00:07:21,441 --> 00:07:25,045
In 2017, the company dropped "Motors" from its

188
00:07:25,045 --> 00:07:25,437
name.

189
00:07:25,437 --> 00:07:27,711
Today, it's simply Tesla.

190
00:07:28,261 --> 00:07:30,005
So here's the full circle.

191
00:07:30,005 --> 00:07:32,540
A man who died broke, in debt, alone in a

192
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:33,820
hotel room.

193
00:07:34,070 --> 00:07:36,524
And more than a century after he patented his

194
00:07:36,524 --> 00:07:38,979
motor, his name became one of the most famous

195
00:07:38,979 --> 00:07:40,670
brands in the world.

196
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:42,498
No way.

197
00:07:43,248 --> 00:07:45,818
If you want more true stories that sound made

198
00:07:45,818 --> 00:07:46,617
up, subscribe.

199
00:07:46,617 --> 00:07:49,062
And if you liked this one, watch another one

200
00:07:49,062 --> 00:07:51,007
next, because they're just as wild.

